The original C3 implementation confirms the needed seam: full-batch computes one gradient over all size≥2 training rows, while minibatching can call the same sealed head’s scalar update on subsets. I will evaluate the full training objective at every checkpoint in both arms; minibatch loss from the last small batch will not substitute for the common objective.

Before running, I’m freezing the remaining procedural choices: batch size 256; per-fold deterministic shuffles without replacement within each pass; every remainder batch is used; the row stream reshuffles for the next pass; model initialisation remains the sealed seed, and shuffling gets an independent domain-separated seed. The original four-part rule will compare each cadence point with the immediately preceding 100-update point.
